WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.340
[BREATHES DEEP] Aaaaaah!

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.020
Don’t you just love that new car smell?

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:09.980
Smells like power and freedom!

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.850
Actually, it smells more like petroleum-based
solvents evaporating off vinyl and plastic.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:15.980
Wow, buzzkill.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:21.020
Let’s see if you still like that smell when
you find out what it's really costing you...

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:22.860
I’m locking the windows.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.780
[SOUND OF WINDOWS ROLLING UP]

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:32.400
[MUSIC]

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.520
Since the auto boom of the 1950's, cars have
become an integral part of American society.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:44.890
Car companies spent 14.2 BILLION dollars in
advertising alone in 2014 and that number

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:45.890
is only rising.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:50.640
They convince us that once we’re behind
the wheel, our families will love us more,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.010
our neighbors will envy us, and our freedom
to go anywhere is secure.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.679
Unfortunately, they’re more likely to take
away your freedom and security.

00:00:59.679 --> 00:01:02.059
New cars are a financial triple threat.

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:07.390
We borrow money at interest, to buy an asset
that we have to PAY to maintain and...here’s

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.560
the big kicker...that drastically depreciates
in value.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.600
Wait… what IS depreciation, anyway?

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.880
Hey Julia, would you like to buy some ice
cream?

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.600
Uh, sure, I guess--

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:18.640
That’ll be four dollars.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.460
I wouldn’t pay a nickel for that!

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:24.610
But I paid four dollars for it twenty minutes
ago!

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.869
That’s depreciation.

00:01:26.869 --> 00:01:28.720
Go wash your hands.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.760
Cars retain their value about as well as ice
cream on a hot day.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.259
A new car will depreciate 63% in the first
five years.

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:41.770
10% of that the MOMENT you drive it off the
lot!

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:46.380
Calling cars a “bad investment” is like
calling a honey badger a bad heart surgeon.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:47.380
What?

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.680
I mean they’re not an investment at all!

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:51.680
Exactly.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.579
I mean, can you think of anything else you’d
spend that much money on that loses value

00:01:56.579 --> 00:01:57.579
that quickly?

00:01:57.579 --> 00:01:58.579
Oh, I don’t know.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.840
How about a garage full of beanie babies?

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.100
[HORROR MOVIE MUSIC]

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.080
Heh, I told you the 90’s are comin’ back.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:09.820
Sure, hon.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:14.200
And don't think you can get out of this by
leasing either!

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.140
Leasing companies set their prices so that
you pay for the depreciation of their vehicle,

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.690
and when it’s over they still have an asset
they can re-sell.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:26.819
So let’s set a few simple ground rules that
will put you in the driver’s seat instead

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:28.750
of being taken for a ride:

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:29.750
Number One.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.260
Buy a car that’s 5 years old.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:36.250
Right away, you’re skipping the majority
of its depreciation.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:37.250
Number Two.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.920
Save up to buy a car in cash.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.810
Paying interest on something that loses value
is like gaining weight from exercising.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:45.810
All pain.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:46.810
No gain.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:47.810
Number Three.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:52.680
If you can't comfortably afford to save an
amount equal to your car payment, you can't

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:53.680
afford it.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.920
So if you're not saving $300 a month, you
shouldn’t take on a $300 car payment.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.090
So how much money is actually at stake?

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:02.980
I think it’s time to....

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.099
RUN THE NUMBERS!

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.020
Let’s say I buy a new car for $20,000.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.830
I put $4,000 down, and I finance the rest
over 60 months at 4.25%.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:17.160
My monthly payment will be--

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.840
$295.42!

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:20.989
That sounds pretty normal.

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:22.180
But check this out.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.439
What if you bought that same car, but a 5
year old model, which costs you 63% less -- or

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:28.439
$7,400.

00:03:28.439 --> 00:03:32.810
Put your same $4,000 down, and with the same
loan arrangement, you’re looking at a payment

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.020
of just $62.78/mo.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:37.380
That’s a monthly saving of--

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:40.960
$232.65!

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.920
I could get a massage every other week with
that!

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:44.980
You could.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.579
Or, you could take those monthly savings and
put them into a growing asset, like a home

00:03:49.579 --> 00:03:50.590
or a mutual fund.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:55.489
And if you continued to do that EVERY time
you paid off the car, even at a very conservative

00:03:55.489 --> 00:04:00.140
7% per year return, in 35 years you’d have
over...

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:03.400
$421,459!

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:04.260
Really?

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.360
Wow.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:11.159
Now...is that enough to retire on?

00:04:11.159 --> 00:04:12.159
Probably not.

00:04:12.159 --> 00:04:16.989
But that’s just one thing you can start
doing now to prepare for your future.

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:19.050
There are many, many more.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:24.220
Y’know, as much I like that new car smell,
I don’t think I’d spend half a mil on it.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.180
Let's try this instead.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.200
Ooh, what’s that?

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.360
EARLY RETIREMENT, BABY! Bam!

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.000
And that’s our two cents!

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.340
[MUSIC]

